---
export const prerender = true;
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/ui/Icon.astro";

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  "headline": "Google Takeout JSON Sidecars and Photo Metadata",
  "description": "A plain-language guide to the extra files in a Google Photos download, what details they may contain, and how to check them safely.",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "Rahul Jena",
    "url": "https://rahuljena.is-a.dev/"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "TakeoutFix",
    "url": "https://takeoutfix.pages.dev",
    "logo": "https://takeoutfix.pages.dev/favicon-192x192.png"
  },
  "datePublished": "2026-09-15",
  "dateModified": "2026-10-14",
  "articleSection": "Photo dates and details"
};
---

<Layout
  title="Google Takeout Extra Photo Files: A Simple Guide | TakeoutFix"
  description="Learn what the extra .json files in a Google Photos download may contain, how to match them with photos, and how to restore dates or locations safely."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 mt-12 font-sans text-zinc-300">
    
    <!-- Breadcrumb & Metadata Header -->
    <div class="mb-12">
      <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-zinc-500 font-mono mb-4">
        <a href="/" class="hover:text-zinc-300 transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-zinc-300 transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <span class="text-zinc-400">Extra .json files</span>
      </nav>

      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-indigo-500/10 border border-indigo-500/20 text-indigo-500 dark:text-indigo-400 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider mb-4 font-mono">
        <Icon name="file-text" class="w-3.5 h-3.5" />
        Photo file guide
      </div>

      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mb-6 leading-tight max-w-4xl">
        Google Takeout Extra Photo Files: A Simple Guide
      </h1>

      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 text-xs text-zinc-500 font-mono pb-6 border-b border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800">
        <span>By <strong>Rahul Jena</strong></span>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>Published September 2026 · Updated October 2026</span>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>About 5 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 2-Column Responsive Layout -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-10">
      
      <!-- Main Editorial Content (lg:col-span-8) -->
      <article class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-10 text-base leading-relaxed font-normal">
        
        <!-- Executive Summary Box -->
        <div class="bg-indigo-500/5 border border-indigo-500/20 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
            <Icon name="info" class="w-5 h-5 text-indigo-500" />
            Executive Summary
          </h2>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            A Google Photos download may include extra .json files beside some photos. This guide explains what they are, how to tell which photo they belong to, and what to do if a photo's date or location looks wrong.
          </p>
        </div>


        <section id="why-json" class="space-y-4">
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white">
            1. What are the extra files for?
          </h2>
          <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            These are small details files that may sit beside a photo or video. Their names often start with the photo's name and end in <code>.json</code>. The names and contents can vary, so compare the full names of nearby files. You do not need to open or edit these files to use TakeoutFix.
          </p>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            <li>Google says extra details, such as comments added in Google Photos, may be saved in a separate JSON file.</li>
            <li>Because the details file is separate, another photo app may not read it automatically.</li>
            <li>Not every photo has one of these files. The information available depends on your download.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            Read <a href="https://support.google.com/photos/answer/3024190" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline font-semibold">Google Photos Help: download your data</a> for Google's current instructions. What appears in your own download may differ.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="anatomy-schema" class="space-y-4">
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white">
            2. What details might be included?
          </h2>
          <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            Some details files include the photo's name, the date it was taken, or its location. Others may contain different information or no details that can be restored. A file ending in <code class="text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-200 bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-xs">.json</code> is a clue that it may be a details file; its full name helps match it to the photo.
          </p>
          <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            In simple terms, a details file may tell the tool:
          </p>

          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            <li>The photo or video the file belongs to.</li>
            <li>When it was taken, if that date is available.</li>
            <li>Where it was taken, if a location is available.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">The extra file might not have every detail. TakeoutFix only restores information it can find, so some photos may stay unchanged.</p>
        </section>


        <section id="why-programs-fail" class="space-y-4">
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white">
            3. Why might another app show a different date?
          </h2>
          <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            A photo can have a date saved inside the photo and a different date in a separate details file. Some apps use the date inside the photo and ignore the extra file, so the photo may appear under the wrong day after import. App behavior can vary; try a few restored photos first.
          </p>
          <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            Your computer may also show when a file was downloaded or copied. Keep your original download and check a small group of photos in the app you plan to use before moving your whole library.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="restoration-mechanism" class="space-y-4">
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white">
            4. What happens when you restore a photo?
          </h2>
          <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            TakeoutFix updates available photo details without changing the visible picture. It works only with supported file types and information present in your download. Keep the original files as a backup.
          </p>
          <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            The process is simple:
          </p>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            <li>It looks for a matching details file.</li>
            <li>It adds the date or location that is available to a supported photo or video.</li>
            <li>You review the results and keep the original files until you are satisfied.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <section id="solution-comparison" class="space-y-4">
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white">
            5. Which TakeoutFix option should I use?
          </h2>
          <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
            Use the browser tool for a typical archive. If you have an exceptionally large library, see the desktop app page for its requirements and limits.
          </p>
          <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/50 p-5">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mb-2">Use the browser tool</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Best for most libraries. Choose the Takeout folder and save restored copies on your device.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/50 p-5">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mb-2">Use the desktop app</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Consider it for very large libraries. Check the download page for supported computers and current limits.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>


        <!-- Conclusion & CTA -->
        <section class="bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/50 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 rounded-2xl p-8 text-center shadow-sm">
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mb-3">Put your photo dates and locations back</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 max-w-xl mx-auto mb-6">
            The browser tool processes selected media locally. Keep your original export and review a sample of the output before you migrate or delete anything.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 justify-center">
            <a href="/tool" class="px-6 py-3 rounded-xl bg-zinc-900 dark:bg-white text-white dark:text-zinc-900 hover:opacity-90 font-semibold text-sm transition-opacity shadow-sm">
              Launch Restore Tool &rarr;
            </a>
            <a href="/guides" class="px-6 py-3 rounded-xl bg-white dark:bg-zinc-800 hover:bg-zinc-100 dark:hover:bg-zinc-700 text-zinc-900 dark:text-white font-semibold text-sm border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-700 transition-colors shadow-sm">
              Browse All Guides
            </a>
          </div>
        </section>

      </article>

      <!-- Sticky Table of Contents & Quick Action Sidebar (lg:col-span-4) -->
      <aside class="lg:col-span-4 space-y-6">
        <div class="sticky top-28 space-y-6">
          
          <div class="bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/50 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
              <Icon name="book-open" class="w-4 h-4 text-zinc-900 dark:text-white" />
              Table of Contents
            </h3>
            <nav class="space-y-2 text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">
              <a href="#why-json" class="block hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors py-1">1. What are these extra files?</a>
              <a href="#anatomy-schema" class="block hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors py-1">2. What details might they contain?</a>
              <a href="#why-programs-fail" class="block hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors py-1">3. Why does an app show a different date?</a>
              <a href="#restoration-mechanism" class="block hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors py-1">4. What happens during restore?</a>
              <a href="#solution-comparison" class="block hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors py-1">5. Which option should I use?</a>
            </nav>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-zinc-900 dark:bg-white text-white dark:text-zinc-900 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-md">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-base mb-2">Restore your photos</h4>
            <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-zinc-300 dark:text-zinc-600 mb-4">
              Check whether dates and locations can be restored on your device.
            </p>
            <a 
              href="/tool" 
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-semibold px-4 py-2.5 rounded-xl bg-white/10 dark:bg-zinc-100 hover:bg-white/20 dark:hover:bg-zinc-200 transition-colors"
            >
              Open Restore Tool &rarr;
            </a>
          </div>

        </div>
      </aside>

    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
